  "notes": "## Signal Pulse — Website performance, 2026-04-08 → 2026-07-07 (91 days)\n\n_First Pulse run — `analytics-data.js` was the empty template, so there is no prior snapshot to diff. This run sets the baseline; run-over-run arrows populate next run. Where trend is shown below it is **within-window** (first vs. second half of the 91 days), labeled as such. Source: a **Plausible** export of sternsecurity.com (the drop was Plausible, not GA4 — same metric families: visits≈sessions, visitors≈users, pageviews). It is **website-only**: no LinkedIn post export was present, so post/format/hook analysis is at the website-content level, and LinkedIn appears only as a referral channel. Every figure below is read directly from the exports; \"engaged-visit rate\" = 100 − Plausible bounce rate._\n\n### Totals\n- **Visits (sessions): 3,430 · Unique visitors: 3,276 · Pageviews (impressions): 4,631**\n- **Avg engagement rate: 24.1%** (engaged-visit share = 100 − site bounce 75.9%, visits-weighted) · avg visit duration 54.7s\n- Channels: Direct 1,693 · Organic Search 1,421 · Referral 101 · Organic Social 80 · AI Assistants 12 · Email 3\n- Top sources: Google 1,293 · Bing 66 · LinkedIn 46 · DuckDuckGo 24 · Brave 23 · cisa.gov 12 · healthcarebreaches.com 9 · ChatGPT 5 · Perplexity 4\n- Goal conversions: Outbound link clicks 153 · **404 = 93** ⚠ · Visit /contact/ 75 · Demo Button 17 · WP Search 11 · Login 9 · File Download 2\n\n### What's working\n- **Comparison / \"X vs Y\" is the top format by reach.** `/blog/red-team-vs-blue-team-vs-purple-team…/` is the #1 content page (315 visitors); the Comparison/explainer format pulls 419 visitors across 3 pieces — the most of any format. The hook that works is the definitional head-to-head (\"red vs blue vs purple\", \"authenticated vs unauthenticated\", \"pen test vs vuln scan\").\n- **Compliance/framework explainers engage best.** `/free-cmmc-level-1/` is the standout on every quality signal — 43% engaged, 90s dwell, 67% scroll, 57% bounce (site is 76%). The Framework/compliance format has the highest engaged rate of any format (23%), and by buying moment **Upcoming Compliance Deadline** is the best-engaging cluster (23% across 164 visitors).\n- **The CRQ / \"risk in dollars\" page over-performs its traffic.** `/velocity/cyber-risk-quantification/` — 52 visitors but 19% engaged, 79s dwell, above site average.\n- **LinkedIn is the highest-quality referral source.** Only 46 visitors, but 50% bounce and 56s duration vs the 76% / 55s site average — LinkedIn traffic is materially more engaged than search traffic.\n- **Education is the workhorse category** (custom prop: Education 831, Tools 392, Frameworks 127, Research 114); Jon Sternstein (2,037) and the Stern Security house byline (1,134) carry authorship.\n\n### What's declining\n- **Overall traffic softened in the back half of the window** — second half (May 23–Jul 7) vs first half: visitors −14.5%, pageviews −18.5%, visits −16.1%.\n- **High-reach technical/comparison posts leak.** `/blog/local-network-attacks-llmnr-and-nbt-ns-poisoning/` (287 visitors, #2) bounces at 95%; the top comparison and technical-how-to posts sit at 91–98% bounce — strong search acquisition, almost no onward journey.\n- **Data reports and case studies barely engage** — MoveIt 0-day impact, Healthcare Data Breach Report 2025, and the large-hospital case study all land at 0–4% engaged; they arrive via search and bounce.\n- **404s are the second-largest goal event** (93 unique) — a real SEO/UX drain from stale inbound links and bad URLs.\n\n### Recommendations\n1. **Own the CRQ narrative — it's a triple-confirmed opening.** Stern's CRQ page engages above average; competitors have vacated the theme (1 vendor, 3 posts, 0.4% of 16-vendor LinkedIn output); and search demand, while low-volume, is rising with near-zero difficulty and high commercial intent (\"cyber risk quantification software\" 170/mo, KD 19, +30.4% YoY, CPC ~$45). Anchor LinkedIn + a site content cluster on \"your cyber risk in dollars.\"\n2. **Keep comparison/explainer as the reach engine, but plug the leak.** Add a next-step CTA / internal link (Velocity, contact, or a related deep-dive) to the top 3 \"X vs Y\" posts so the search traffic they already win has somewhere to go — today those pages bounce 91–98%.\n3. **Scale the CMMC/compliance play — best-engaging content on a live deadline.** Expand around `/free-cmmc-level-1/`. Ride the nuance: DoD suspended CMMC Phase 2 third-party audits (Jul 13 2026) but Phase 1 self-assessment obligations remain — \"what the pause does and doesn't change.\" Add HIPAA long-tails (rising search: \"hipaa compliance electronic health records\" 320/mo, +42.2%).\n4. **UTM-tag every LinkedIn post link.** LinkedIn is your most-engaged referral source yet none of it is tagged (no `linkedin` in utm_sources), so post-level attribution is impossible. Add `utm_source=linkedin&utm_campaign=<post>` so the next Pulse run can tie website conversions to specific posts.\n5. **Fix the 404 leak and instrument AI-assistant referrals.** Audit/redirect the URLs behind the 93 404 events. Separately, AI Assistants (ChatGPT 5, Perplexity 4, Copilot 2, Claude 1) is small but emerging — AIEO-optimize the top comparison/framework pages so LLMs cite Stern.\n\n### Cyber-space trends worth riding\n- **AI-enabled offense.** An attacker AI-breached an AWS environment in 72h (Jul 2026) and \"artificial intelligence penetration testing\" is a rising search term (+31.9%). Stern's \"cyborg AI bypass\" post already lives here — lean into AI-assisted pentest / AI attack-surface content.\n- **Cyber risk in dollars (CRQ).** The uncontested-on-LinkedIn whitespace above, aligned to Stern's Velocity differentiator.\n- **Compliance pressure.** CMMC Phase 2 pause + rising HIPAA search — anxious buyers are actively searching; explainer content converts the anxiety.\n- **Healthcare vertical.** Stern's healthcarebreaches.com referral + healthcare content, and competitors Clearwater/CORL own this vertical — MoveIt/healthcare-breach data reports are on-brand and defensible.\n\n_External context — search demand from stern-keywords (DataForSEO, real data; several roots hit provider errors this run, so only successfully-returned phrases are quoted and nothing is estimated for the gaps — notably CMMC/SOC2/PCI search volume was unavailable). Competitor read from vault-data.js (2026-07-08, 16 vendors, 755 posts): TPRM is the most-posted theme (25.8%) but lowest-engagement (19.2 avg) — a red ocean; CRQ essentially unclaimed. Recent-news context via web search (Jul 2026)._\n",
